WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.990
Even a great movie can be ruined by a bad
ending, it's arguably the hardest, and most

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:07.660
important, part to get right.

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:12.620
Continuity errors and awkward dialogue might
be forgiven, but a movie's ending sticks with

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:14.680
the audience long after the credits roll.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.300
Thankfully, that's what alternate endings
are for.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:22.620
Check out these famous films with awesome
alternate endings, but watch out for spoilers!

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:24.180
Heathers

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:30.939
In the 1988 black comedy Heathers, Veronica,
played by Winona Ryder, learns a lesson about

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:35.680
the high cost of popularity after she joins
a clique of girls, all named Heather, at her

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:36.180
school.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:41.100
She also becomes romantically involved with
new student JD, played by Christian Slater,

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.320
who is determined to shake up the social status
quo in some very unexpected ways.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:46.760
Like with bombs.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:52.110
In the theatrical ending, after JD's bomb
explodes, Veronica walks into the school,

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.600
where she encounters Heather Duke.

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.660
Veronica takes away Heather's red scrunchie
and tells her…

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.700
“Heather my love, there’s a new sheriff
in town.”

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:06.619
Then she catches up to her old friend Martha
"Dumptruck" Dunnstock, and the film ends on

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:07.520
a hopeful note.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.540
Several other endings to Heathers were scrapped
along the way.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:19.700
“The original ending was that the bomb was
strapped to me and I give this amazing speech.”

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:25.220
In the best alternate version, writer Daniel
Waters gives Martha Dunnstock the last laugh.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:29.860
When Veronica tries to rekindle their friendship,
Martha stabs her in the chest, calling her

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:30.360
Heather.

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:34.900
As she dies, Veronica protests that her name
isn’t Heather, but Martha ignores her, getting

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.360
up from her wheelchair and triumphantly walking
away from the friend that betrayed her.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:39.860
Ouch.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:41.760
The Butterfly Effect

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:48.079
In the 2004 psychological thriller The Butterfly
Effect, 20-year-old Evan discovers his frequent

00:01:48.079 --> 00:01:52.869
headaches and blackouts are actually a manifestation
of his ability to travel back in time.

00:01:52.869 --> 00:01:58.079
Evan uses his childhood journals to go back
and change traumatic experiences for himself

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:58.800
and his friends.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:03.399
However, he soon learns that even small and
seemingly positive changes can drastically

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.100
affect the future, and not always in a good
way.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:10.920
In the theatrical ending, Evan travels back
to the day he met his friend Kayleigh and

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.480
changes things so they never meet and become
neighbors.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.900
When Evan returns to the future, he's relieved
to see that this change has made things better

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:19.100
for the lives of his friends.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:23.860
He burns his journals, thereby preventing
himself from ever altering the timeline again.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:28.980
The alternate ending found on the Director's
Cut DVD is much darker than the original.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.000
On the brink of madness, Evan travels back
in time, strangling himself in the womb with

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.620
his own umbilical cord, to prevent his own
birth.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.700
For a movie that offers so much to think about,
this ending is definitely the more thought-provoking

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:42.200
one.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:43.800
First Blood

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:49.580
In the 1982 action film First Blood, Vietnam
veteran John Rambo drifts through Washington

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:52.400
state, where he has a run-in with an intolerant
Sheriff.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:57.451
After the former Green Beret refuses to be
pushed around by the authoritarian cop, Rambo

00:02:57.451 --> 00:03:01.659
finds himself fighting for his life as he
tries to escape the small town and evade the

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:03.000
lawman and his lackeys.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.920
At the end, Rambo corners and wounds the sheriff,
preparing to kill him.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:11.580
However, his former commander Colonel Sam
Trautman arrives in time to stop John from

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:16.780
doing the deed and Rambo breaks down, recounting
his traumatic experiences during the war and

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:18.120
surrendering to Trautman.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:23.180
It's not necessarily a bad ending, but it
was a little unsatisfying, and it left the

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:25.660
door open for several mediocre sequels.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:30.940
The original ending to First Blood keeps much
closer to David Morrell's 1972 novel.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:35.640
Rambo still has his tearful breakdown, but
he decides that the events of Vietnam have

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:39.800
made his life all but pointless, as he can't
live normally in society anymore.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.860
Rambo gives the Colonel his gun.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:43.120
“Take me."

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:47.680
"You owe me that.

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:50.880
Do it.”

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:55.820
When Trautman can't do it, Rambo grabs his
arm, causing the gun to go off.

00:03:56.380 --> 00:04:00.469
Given what we know of Rambo's character, this
finale seems like a more fitting and realistic

00:04:00.469 --> 00:04:01.680
way for him to go out.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:03.500
Pretty In Pink

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:09.980
John Hughes' 1986 cult classic Pretty In Pink
features geeky high schoolers Andie and her

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:11.180
best friend, Duckie.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:16.030
Duckie is madly in love with Andie, but she
begins dating the popular Blane, triggering

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:17.640
a bitter argument with Duckie.

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:22.320
Later, Blane's friend Steff teases him about
Andie, causing Blane to avoid her and lie

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.680
about his prom plans.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:28.580
Undeterred, Andie goes to the prom alone,
but she still has a chance to find true love.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:32.340
At the prom, Andie and Duckie make up and
head for the dance floor.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.740
After telling Steff what he thinks of him,
Blane congratulates Duckie and tells Andie

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:39.920
how he feels about her, kissing her on the
cheek and walking out.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:41.020
“I love you”

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:52.180
Duckie puts his own feelings aside and encourages
Andie to follow him.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:56.680
Andie and Blane kiss in the parking lot, Duckie
dances with a cute girl, and they all live

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:57.900
happily ever after.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:01.380
It's all very romantic, if you're a 13-year-old
girl.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:06.360
Test audiences, as it turns out, didn't like
the original ending, which had Andie choose

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:07.410
Duckie over Blane.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:11.620
Pretty in Pink should have been about two
geeks finding love, but the revised ending

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:16.180
turns it into a formulaic teen fairytale,
except Blane is no Prince Charming.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:17.700
"Hey let's go upstairs, huh?"

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:23.900
Jon Cryer may be famous for that dance in
the record store, but the scene where Duckie

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:28.780
sits in his room listening to Morrissey is
the most honest performance of teenage heartbreak

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:29.560
ever filmed.

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:32.180
Scott Pilgrim vs. The World

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:38.330
In Edgar Wright's 2010 action comedy Scott
Pilgrim vs. The World, slacker musician Scott

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:41.080
Pilgrim flounders his way through an unsatisfying
life.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:46.130
He's a bassist in a struggling garage band,
he has no plans for the future, and his girlfriend,

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:49.400
Knives Chau, is a clingy 17-year-old high
school student.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:54.380
When Scott meets Ramona, he immediately becomes
infatuated with her, but he must defeat her

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:57.020
seven evil exes in order to win her heart.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:01.490
The ending used for the theatrical release
was actually supposed to be the movie's alternate

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:01.990
ending.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:06.060
It was shot after the original one had been
filmed in order to match the ending of the

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:07.680
Scott Pilgrim graphic novels.

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:12.419
After helping Scott defeat Ramona's final
evil ex, Knives tells Scott that she's come

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:15.700
to terms with their breakup and understands
that their relationship is over.

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:19.660
Although she swore to win him back, Knives
gives her blessing for Scott to pursue his

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:20.980
new romance with Ramona.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:25.760
In the film's original ending, Scott realizes
that he still has feelings for Knives.

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:27.300
“Your hair’s getting really shaggy."

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:29.000
"It is?"

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:32.820
"I like it."

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:34.760
"You do?"

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:39.860
The couple rekindle their romance over a video
game, and Ramona realizes that she doesn't

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:43.540
need to be in a relationship or constantly
reinventing herself to be happy.

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:47.520
Seeing healthy and mature decision-making
in a movie is like seeing a unicorn in the

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.820
wild, which is why we love this ending.

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:52.220
Die Hard with a Vengeance

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:58.470
In 1995's Die Hard with a Vengeance, terrorist
Simon Gruber has taken all of New York City

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:03.840
hostage in order to steal a boatload of gold
and to get revenge on Lieutenant John McClane

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:05.530
for the death of his brother, Hans.

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:10.819
With the help of Harlem shopkeeper Zeus Carver,
McClane deals with bomb threats and bank heists

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:14.240
as he pursues Gruber and his lackeys across
the five boroughs.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:18.280
At the film’s climax, McClane follows Gruber
to a hideout in Quebec.

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:23.039
Most of the bad guys are quickly rounded up,
but Gruber and his girlfriend attempt to escape

00:07:23.039 --> 00:07:24.220
in a helicopter.

00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:26.020
McClane manages to get the best of them.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:29.900
"Get out of here!"

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:40.480
It's a typical action-film finale: unrealistic,
full of questionable physics, and made to

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:42.140
showcase a big explosion.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:44.200
"Yippee kay yay, mother----er!"

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:49.340
In an alternate ending, Gruber escapes with
the gold, while McClane takes the blame for

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:51.100
the terrorist's trail of destruction.

00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:56.380
The NYPD kicks McClane off the force, but
he goes rogue and tracks down Simon Gruber

00:07:56.380 --> 00:07:56.880
himself.

00:07:57.380 --> 00:08:00.020
“How are my friends in the NYPD?”

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:02.580
"Well, I was fired from the NYPD.

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:04.940
Cops trying to save my pension."

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:06.460
“I’m sorry to hear that.”

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:11.830
He corners the villain, forcing him to play
a game of "McClane Says," with riddles and

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:13.700
a rocket launcher thrown in for fun.

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:18.580
After a few suspenseful rounds, Gruber loses,
taking a RPG to the chest.

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:22.879
This more realistic ending is miles better
than the theatrical version.

00:08:22.879 --> 00:08:26.420
Sometimes, a small "boom" is even more satisfying
than a big one.

